Can the algorithms return different edge sets?
Yes. An MST is not always unique. When two edges have equal weights, different tie-breaking decisions can produce different valid MSTs.
- Kruskal may select A–B before A–C when the edges have equal weight.
- Prim may select A–C or A–B depending on its starting vertex and tie-breaking rule.
- Borůvka may also select either edge when a component has multiple cheapest outgoing edges.
Both edge sets are correct because they connect all six vertices, contain five edges, have no cycles and have the same minimum total cost.

Observation: The edge set may differ, but the minimum total cost remains the same.
6. Industry decision – Choosing an algorithm
Environment	Suitable algorithm	Reason based on behaviour
A. Sparse network	Kruskal	When there are relatively few edges, sorting the edge list is manageable. Disjoint sets efficiently detect cycles while joining separate components.
B. Dense network	Prim	A dense network has many possible connections. Prim can grow one tree by repeatedly choosing the cheapest edge crossing from visited to unvisited vertices. An adjacency matrix makes finding the minimum candidate straightforward.
C. Large network using parallel/distributed resources	Borůvka	Different components can independently find their cheapest outgoing edges. These choices can be processed in parallel and merged in rounds, making the algorithm suitable for distributed processing.

Important implementation characteristics
Kruskal
- Works with a sorted list of edges.
- Uses Disjoint Set Union (DSU) for cycle detection.
- Convenient when the network has fewer edges.
- Sorting the edges is its main preparation step.
Prim
- Maintains one growing connected tree.
- Uses a priority queue with an adjacency list, or an adjacency matrix for dense graphs.
- Useful when the network has many edges and a connected tree can be grown efficiently.
- Its behaviour depends on the starting vertex, although the final MST cost remains minimum.
Borůvka
- Maintains several connected components.
- Each component searches for its cheapest outgoing edge.
- Multiple component searches can happen simultaneously.
- Useful when the network is large and edge processing can be distributed across machines.
7. Complexity comparison
Let V represent the number of vertices and E represent the number of edges.
Algorithm	Typical time complexity	Main data structure
Kruskal	\(O(E\log E)\)	Sorted edge list and DSU
Prim	\(O(E\log V)\) with adjacency list and priority queue	Adjacency list and priority queue
Borůvka	\(O(E\log V)\)	Edge list and DSU

The actual performance also depends on graph density, memory usage, sorting cost, and whether the work can be performed in parallel.
8. Result
Kruskal’s, Prim’s and Borůvka’s algorithms were implemented using the same connected weighted graph. All three constructed a Minimum Spanning Tree with five edges and a total cost of 9.

The modified graph demonstrated that multiple MSTs can have different edge sets but the same minimum total cost. Kruskal is convenient for sparse networks, Prim is suitable for dense networks, and Borůvka is useful for large networks where parallel processing is possible.
